            {
              "id": "glog-fb5c152e-afe3-4249-a091-78cb31224f37",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the source string is larger than the destination buffer. This can lead to arbitrary code execution, denial of service, or information disclosure vulnerabilities.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `strcpy` is used to copy the string from `binaryFilename_vgg_str.c_str()` to `binaryFilename_vgg`. If the size of the string returned by `binaryFilename_vgg_str.c_str()` is larger than the buffer `binaryFilename_vgg`, it can lead to a buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string copy functions that check the size of the destination buffer. In C++, this can be achieved by using the `strncpy` function, which takes an additional parameter specifying the maximum number of characters to be copied from the source string.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcpy` function with `strncpy` as follows:\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(binaryFilename_vgg, binaryFilename_vgg_str.c_str(), sizeof(binaryFilename_vgg) - 1);\nbinaryFilename_vgg[sizeof(binaryFilename_vgg) - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null termination\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstring>`: This library is required for the `strcpy` and `strncpy` functions.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
